<<<
differentiation between that & which, & restoration of that to the place from which, in print, it tends to be ousted.
===
differentiation between *that* & *which,* & restoration of *that* to the place from which, in print, it tends to be ousted.
>>>

<<<
distinction is that that is the colloquial & which the literary relative.
===
distinction is that *that* is the colloquial & *which* the literary relative.
>>>

<<<
that the proportion of thats to whichs is far higher in speech than in writing; but the reason is not that the spoken thats are properly converted into written whichs, but that the kind of clause properly begun with
===
that the proportion of *that*s to *which*s is far higher in speech than in writing; but the reason is not that the spoken *that*s are properly converted into written *which*s, but that the kind of clause properly begun with
>>>

<<<
 which is rare in speech with its short detached sentences, but very com- -mon in the more complex & continuous structure of writing, while the kind properly begun with that is equally necessary in both.
===
 *which* is rare in speech with its short detached sentences, but very common in the more complex & continuous structure of writing, while the kind properly begun with *that* is equally necessary in both.
>>>

<<<
the original that of their thoughts into a which for presentation in print.
===
the original *that* of their thoughts into a *which* for presentation in print.
>>>

<<<
to one of which that & to the other of which which is appropriate, are the defining & the non-defining; & if writers would agree to regard that as the defining relative pronoun, & which as the non-defining, there would be much gain both in lucidity & in ease.
===
to one of which *that* & to the other of which *which* is appropriate, are the defining & the non-defining; & if writers would agree to regard *that* as the defining relative pronoun, & *which* as the non-defining, there would be much gain both in lucidity & in ease.
>>>

<<<
A defining relative clause 1s one that identifies the person or thing meant by limiting the denotation of the antecedent: Each made a list of books that had influenced him; not books generally, but books as defined by the that-clause.
===
A defining relative clause is one that identifies the person or thing meant by limiting the denotation of the antecedent: *Each made a list of books that had influenced him*; not books generally, but books as defined by the that-clause.
>>>

<<<
Contrast with that: I always buy his books, which have influenced me_ greatly; the clause does not limit his books,
===
Contrast with that: *I always buy his books, which have influenced me greatly*; the clause does not limit *his books,*
>>>

<<<
the practice of using that if it is, & which if it is not, would also be easy but for certain peculiarities of that.
===
the practice of using *that* if it is, & *which* if it is not, would also be easy but for certain peculiarities of *that.*
>>>

<<<
it cannot, like whom & which, endure that a preposition
===
it cannot, like *whom* & *which,* endure that a preposition
>>>

<<<
the closer connexion between a defining, (or that-) clause & the antecedent than between a non-defining (or which-) clause & the antecedent;
===
the closer connexion between a defining, (or *that-*) clause & the antecedent than between a non-defining (or *which-*) clause & the antecedent;
>>>

<<<
but it forces the writer to choose between ending his sentence or clause with a preposition, & giving up that for which. In the article PREPOSITION AT END it is explained
===
but it forces the writer to choose between ending his sentence or clause with a preposition, & giving up *that* for *which.* In the article ^^PREPOSITION AT END^^ it is explained
>>>

<<<
the fact accepted that the preposition-governed case of that is borrowed from which, & its possessive from who;
===
the fact accepted that the preposition-governed case of *that* is borrowed from *which,* & its possessive from *who*;
>>>

<<<
its cases are, then: subj. that; obj. that; poss. whose; prep.-preceded (in, by, from, for, &c.) which.
===
its cases are, then: subj. *that*; obj. *that*; poss. *whose*; prep.-preceded (*in, by, from, for,* &c.) *which.*
>>>

<<<
Another peculiarity of that is that in the defining clauses to which it is proper it may, if it is not the subject, be omitted & yet operative (The man you saw means the same as The man that you saw), while
===
Another peculiarity of *that* is that in the defining clauses to which it is proper it may, if it is not the subject, be omitted & yet operative (*The man you saw* means the same as *The man that you saw*), while
>>>

<<<
 which in the non-defining clauses to which it is proper must be expressed (This fact, which you admit, condemns you cannot be changed without altering the sense to This fact, you admit, condemns you).
===
 *which* in the non-defining clauses to which it is proper must be expressed (*This fact, which you admit, condemns you* cannot be changed without altering the sense to *This fact, you admit, condemns you*).
>>>

